# Build Steps: Priority Email Queue — next-node-ecom

Based on actually reading your repo. Reference code is in the zip already
shared; this is the step order to apply it.

---

## What's already true about your repo (no need to re-check)

- [x] `config/mailer.js` already sends mail via `sendMail({to, subject, html})`
- [x] `utils/emailTemplates.js` already has all 4 templates you need
- [x] `config/redis.js` exists but uses `node-redis`, not `ioredis`
- [x] 5 places currently send email fire-and-forget with no retry:
      `auth.controller.js` (4 spots), `payment.controller.js` (1),
      `order.controller.js` (1)
- [x] Redis in `docker-compose.yml` is configured as an LRU cache
      (`--maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`) — unsafe for a queue as-is
- [x] No newsletter/bulk email exists anywhere — only transactional mail
- [x] `server.js` already runs background work in-process (the
      `expirePendingOrders` interval) — same pattern applies here

---

## Engineering rules for every step below

- [ ] Reuse `sendMail()` and the existing templates — don't recreate them
- [ ] One queue, not two — no bulk tier until a bulk feature exists
- [ ] One process — no separate worker deploy target for now
- [ ] Match existing file/naming style in `backend/src`
- [ ] Small, reviewable change per step — confirm each step works before
      moving to the next

---

## Step 1 — Fix the Redis eviction risk first

Do this before any queue code exists, so there's nothing to lose yet.

- [ ] Decide: dedicated second Redis container (recommended) vs.
      switching the existing Redis to `noeviction` + more memory (simpler,
      but affects the cache's behavior when full)
- [ ] Apply whichever you chose in `docker-compose.yml`
- [ ] Confirm both containers/the single container start healthy with
      `make dev-up`

**Check:** Redis is reachable and its eviction policy no longer risks
dropping queue data.

---

## Step 2 — Install dependencies

- [ ] `npm install bullmq ioredis` inside `backend/`
- [ ] Confirm `backend/package.json` lists both

**Check:** `npm ls bullmq ioredis` shows both installed.

---

## Step 3 — Add the queue Redis connection

- [ ] Add `queueRedisUrl` to `config/env.js`, reading `QUEUE_REDIS_URL`,
      falling back to the existing `redisUrl` if unset
- [ ] Add a new `config/queueRedis.js` — a dedicated `ioredis` connection
      for BullMQ, separate from the existing cache client

**Check:** connection can be created without throwing when the app
boots (log a line confirming it, same as the existing Redis/SMTP log
lines in `server.js`).

---

## Step 4 — Add the priority + queue definition

- [ ] Add `queues/email.priority.js` — the 4 email types this app
      actually sends, each mapped to a priority number
- [ ] Add `queues/email.queue.js` — the queue itself plus one
      `enqueueEmail(type, to, data, opts)` function everything else calls

**Check:** from a throwaway script, `enqueueEmail(...)` resolves without
error and a job shows up in Redis (`redis-cli` or a quick job-count
check).

---

## Step 5 — Add the worker

- [ ] Add `queues/email.worker.js` — maps each email type straight to
      its existing template function and calls the existing `sendMail()`
- [ ] Start it from `server.js`, in `bootstrap()`, next to the existing
      `expirePendingOrders()` setup — not a separate script

**Check:** `enqueueEmail(...)` from Step 4's test now results in an
actual email landing in Mailpit, and a "sent" line in the worker log.

---

## Step 6 — Replace the 4 call sites in `auth.controller.js`

- [ ] Remove the local `queueMail()` helper and its now-unused imports
- [ ] Replace the verification-email call in `sendVerificationEmail()`
- [ ] Replace the reset-email call in `forgotPassword`
- [ ] Replace the password-changed call in `resetPassword`
- [ ] Replace the password-changed call in `changePassword`

**Check:** signup, forgot-password, reset-password, and change-password
flows each still trigger the right email, now via the queue.

---

## Step 7 — Replace the call site in `payment.controller.js`

- [ ] Remove now-unused `sendMail`/template imports
- [ ] Replace the order-confirmed call in the Cashfree webhook handler
- [ ] Add a `jobId` keyed on the order ID, since webhooks can fire more
      than once for the same event

**Check:** trigger the webhook twice for the same order (or simulate a
retry) and confirm only one email actually sends.

---

## Step 8 — Replace the call site in `order.controller.js`

- [ ] Same replacement as Step 7, in the `getMyOrder` fallback path
- [ ] Same `jobId` pattern for dedupe

**Check:** the order-status-check fallback still emails correctly, no
duplicate sends when both this path and the webhook fire for the same
order.

---

## Step 9 — Verify retry behavior

- [ ] Temporarily point `SMTP_HOST` at something invalid, trigger an
      email, confirm the worker retries with backoff instead of failing
      once and giving up
- [ ] Restore `SMTP_HOST`, confirm a manually re-queued job now succeeds

**Check:** a transient SMTP outage no longer means a silently lost
email — it retries and recovers.

---

## Step 10 — Clean up

- [ ] Search the backend for any remaining direct `sendMail(...)` calls
      outside the worker — there should be none
- [ ] Remove any now-dead imports left behind in the 3 controller files
- [ ] Update `.env.development` / prod secrets with `QUEUE_REDIS_URL` if
      you went with the dedicated-container option

**Check:** `grep -rn "sendMail(" backend/src` only shows the definition
in `config/mailer.js` and the one call inside `queues/email.worker.js`.

---

## Explicitly not doing right now

- [ ] No bulk/newsletter queue — nothing sends bulk email yet
- [ ] No dashboard (Bull Board) — not needed at this volume
- [ ] No separate worker process/deploy target — stays in the API process



